# XSH cpow(), cpowf() and cpowl()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Only what the page makes exact is tested: special values (signed zeros,
# infinities, NaN parts), the symmetry rules and the exact results it states.
# The accuracy of other results is not tested. Arguments are built with
# CMPLX() and pass through volatile objects, and the three types (name, name
# with f, name with l) are each tested. The special values on this page carry
# the tag for the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point option and are NOTINUSE
# where it is not claimed.

assertion  XSH.cpow.001
anchor     functions/cpow.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/cpow.html#RETURN VALUE
class      untestable
since      2001
says       cpow computes the complex power of its first argument raised to its
           second, with the branch cut for the first argument along the
           negative real axis, and with the Complex Floating-Point option
           raises the floating-point exceptions that are appropriate for the
           calculation of the parts of the result (spurious ones are allowed).
reason     The page states no special values and no accuracy, and allows
           spurious exceptions, so no exception can be demanded or ruled out;
           the cut position alone fixes no result.
